# Elevator Simulation

> What if elevators could actually think about where they're going?

This started with a completely random thought I had while riding an elevator.

I started thinking that elevators might be much more efficient if they didn't simply react to whoever calls them.

What if they could look at the situation around them where the elevators are, where people are waiting, how far they need to travel;

and make decisions based on the bigger picture?

I didn't have a solution.

I didn't even have a clear idea of how I would turn the thought into an RL problem.

I just couldn't stop thinking about it.

And that's pretty much the point of the playground.

I've been using the playground to take ideas that randomly come to me, turn them into environments, and see what happens.

The guess the character game started from wanting to experiment with agents making decisions through information.

The permutation puzzle came from a game I already played with friends and turned into an experiment around search and heuristics.

This one started much more simply:

I wondered how an elevator could make better decisions, so I had to build an elevator.

---

## The idea

The first version isn't really about RL yet.

I wanted to build the world first.

So right now, there is:

a building with elevators moving between floors,
passengers appearing randomly,
elevators receiving requests,
and the whole thing running as a time based simulation.

The elevators don't just jump from floor to floor either.

They have continuous positions, acceleration, velocity, cruising, braking, and doors that open and close.

The goal was to make something I could actually watch.

Eventually, I want this to become much more than a visual elevator simulator.

I want to be able to create:

different traffic conditions,
compare different ways of dispatching elevators,
measure their performance,
and eventually experiment with learning based approaches.

Rush hour could look different from normal traffic.

A building where most people travel between a few common floors could behave differently from completely random traffic.

Different dispatching strategies could be tested against the exact same passenger patterns.

And somewhere in all of that, there is probably an RL problem.

I just don't know what that problem looks like yet.

And that's okay.

The idea came first. The solution can come later.

---

## What exists right now

The current version is intentionally simple.

* Configured 10 floor building
* Configured 3 elevators
* Continuous, time based elevator movement
* Acceleration, cruising, and braking distance based deceleration
* Opening and closing elevator doors
* Random passenger generation
* Random origins and destinations
* Elevator requests and assignments
* Passenger waiting and journey time tracking
* Event logging and simulation statistics
* A Pygame renderer for watching the simulation

The current experiment is using a nearest assignment dispatcher and a random traffic generator as the starting point.

They're not supposed to be intelligent.

They're there to give me something to observe and a baseline that I can eventually build on.

---

## Why I built the simulation first

One thing I've been trying to do with the Playground is stop waiting until I completely understand an idea before trying it.

With the permutation puzzle, I didn't know exactly how I was going to make the RL version work when I started.

The same thing happened here.

I still don't have a final RL formulation for this project.

Instead of getting stuck trying to design the perfect state space, action space, and reward function beforehand, I decided to build the environment first.

I wanted to see the problem.

Once you can actually watch three elevators moving around a building while passengers start appearing and waiting, questions become much easier to ask:

* What should an elevator actually consider when choosing a request?
* Is distance enough?
* When should an elevator ignore a nearby request?
* How should multiple passengers affect a decision?
* What happens during a traffic surge?
* Should some requests have higher priority?
* How do we measure whether one strategy is actually better?
* Where does reinforcement learning even fit into all of this?

I don't have all the answers.

That's exactly why I'm experimenting with it.

