## The architecture

The simulation is separated into different systems so that the environment itself isn't tied to the visual interface.

elevator/
├── mechanics/
│   ├── building.py
│   ├── clock.py
│   ├── elevator.py
│   ├── events.py
│   ├── passenger.py
│   ├── request.py
│   ├── states.py
│   └── statistics.py
│
├── settings_configs/
│   ├── config.py
│   ├── dispatcher.py
│   └── traffic.py
│
├── simulation/
│   ├── simulation.py
│   └── elevator_renderer.py
└── main.py


The important separation is between the simulation and the renderer.

The simulation knows where an elevator physically is.

The renderer simply shows it.

The simulation knows when a passenger is waiting.

The renderer shows the passenger.

The simulation controls the physics.

The renderer doesn't.

This means the same environment can eventually be run thousands of times without opening a window, which becomes important if I eventually want to train or benchmark agents.

