## Where I want to take it

There isn't really a fixed endpoint for this project yet.

Some of the things I want to experiment with include:

### Different dispatch strategies

For example:

Random
   ↓
Distance based
   ↓
Load-aware
   ↓
Direction aware
   ↓
Multi request scheduling
   ↓
RL


But I don't want to assume beforehand that this is the correct progression.

I want to build the baselines, run experiments, and see what the actual problem looks like.

### Different traffic conditions

The current traffic is random.

Eventually I want to create controlled scenarios such as:

* Normal traffic
* Rush hour
* Mostly upward traffic
* Mostly downward traffic
* Morning office traffic
* Evening return traffic
* Concentrated requests between certain floors
* Sudden traffic spikes
* Repeated requests from the same floor

Then different dispatching strategies can be compared under the same conditions.

### Benchmarking

Eventually I want to be able to run many simulations and compare things like:

* Average waiting time
* Maximum waiting time
* Total waiting time
* Average journey time
* Elevator utilization
* Number of completed passengers
* Performance under different traffic patterns

At that point, the simulation stops being just something I can watch and becomes an actual experimental environment.

### Reinforcement Learning

This is the part I haven't figured out yet.

I don't know what the final state representation should be.

I don't know whether the action should be choosing an elevator, choosing a destination, scheduling stops, or something completely different.

I don't know what reward formulation will make sense.

And I don't want to pretend that I do.

The whole reason I'm building the simulation is to give myself something concrete to investigate.

Maybe the eventual RL problem will be completely different from what I currently imagine.

